%I A400227 #9 Oct 08 2026 20:47:06
%S A400227 2,3,9,11,22,18,40,38,55,52,91,64,123,97,128,126,199,136,243,180,244,
%T A400227 225,345,228,381,306,399,342,526,328,594,453,569,505,685,486,824,621,
%U A400227 777,644,996,630,1088,806,969,885,1284,835,1348,983,1286,1105,1603,1041,1601,1245,1586,1368
%N A400227 Number of distinct positive distances from the origin to points of (1/n)*Z^2 in the closed disc of radius sqrt(2) that do not occur for (1/m)*Z^2 with any m < n.
%C A400227 The distances at scale n are sqrt(k)/n with k a sum of two squares (A001481) and 1 <= k <= 2*n^2; such a distance is new at n if and only if no prime p dividing n has p^2 dividing k. Indeed sqrt(k)/n = sqrt(j)/m means k*m^2 = j*n^2, and j is then again a sum of two squares inside the disc, so the least such m is n/Product_{p | n} p^min(floor(v_p(k)/2), v_p(n)), which equals n exactly when no p^2 with p | n divides k.
%C A400227 The least scale of a distance divides every scale at which it occurs, so Sum_{d | n} a(d) = B(2*n^2), where B(x) counts the sums of two squares in [1, x]. This is the analog of Sum_{d | n} phi(d) = n for the Farey fractions, with Product_{p | n} (1 - 1/p^2) = A007434(n)/n^2 in the role of phi(n)/n.
%C A400227 The disc matters: with the square [0,1]^2 in its place, sqrt(26)/4 occurs at scales 20, 40, 60 and 68, so the least scale need not divide the others and the divisor sum fails.
%H A400227 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="/A400227/b400227.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..300</a>
%F A400227 a(n) = Sum_{d | n} mu(d)*B(2*n^2/d^2), where B(x) = A102548(x) is the number of sums of two squares k with 1 <= k <= x.
%F A400227 Sum_{d | n} a(d) = A102548(2*n^2).
%F A400227 a(p) = B(2*p^2) - 2 for p prime.
%F A400227 a(n) ~ sqrt(2)*K*A007434(n)/sqrt(log n), where K = 0.7642236... (A064533) is the Landau-Ramanujan constant, from B(x) ~ K*x/sqrt(log x); the ratio is 0.984 at n = 300.
%e A400227 a(2) = 3: the distances at scale 2 are sqrt(k)/2 for k in {1, 2, 4, 5, 8}; of these sqrt(4)/2 = 1 and sqrt(8)/2 = sqrt(2) already occur at scale 1, and 4 and 8 are exactly the k divisible by 2^2.
%o A400227 (PARI) B(x) = #select(c->c, Vec(qfrep([1,0;0,1], x)));
%o A400227 a(n) = sumdiv(n, d, moebius(d)*B(2*n^2/d^2));
%Y A400227 Cf. A000010 (the Farey analog), A001481 (sums of two squares), A007434 (Jordan totient J_2), A064533 (Landau-Ramanujan constant), A102548 (counting function of A001481).
%K A400227 nonn,new
%O A400227 1,1
%A A400227 _Carlo Mitchener_, Sep 21 2026
